welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is David Jackson professor of Spanish  and Portuguese at Yale professor  Jackson's research interests focus on  Portuguese and Brazilian literature's  modernist and Inter arts literature  Portuguese culture in Asia and  ethnomusicology he has written and  edited several books and numerous other  publications today we'll talk with  professor Jackson about his forthcoming  book adverse genres in Fernando Pessoa  welcome professor Jackson thank you it's  a pleasure to be here so tell me who is  Fernando Pessoa Fernando Pessoa was a  portuguese writer active in the first  decades of the 20th century who is a  very unusual figure he's been called the  man who never was for several reasons  but mainly because he sacrificed a  normal life for a literary project in  other words he lived for his literary  creations he was also unusual because he  was brought up in South Africa in  English and his main literary influences  are at first through English literature  and also because for personal reasons he  divided his work into numerous  personalities that he called hetero  names who were other writers to whom he  gave complete biographies and horoscopes  each of him wrote in a different genre  and this fragmented universe constitutes  his work he almost had no biography and  but there is one written by a French  writer that's called strange stranger  which gives some idea of the rather  occult and reclusive figure that  Fernando Pessoa was he never married he  worked at doing commercial translation  for correspondence just enough to  finance his literary projects and he  lived in a series of rented rooms in  Lisbon after his return there in 1905  until his death in 1935 without  virtually having ever left the city yet  he lived universally in his imagination  so for that extent to that extent he's  fascinating figure much better known of  course in translation throughout Europe  still somewhat ifficult figure in the  United States because the lack of good  or enough translations but nonetheless  he appears on pages of The New Yorker  and New York Review of Books and there  are many more English readers who are  now interested in his works okay sounds  like a fascinating individual how did  you come to write a book about him this  is a long story when I was a graduate  student of course I took seminars on  Fernando Pessoa with the great  Portuguese critic and writer social  dissent at the University of Wisconsin  and since pessoa is one of the big  literary names in the literature's of  Portuguese language  I taught seminars on his work from my  earliest days as a professor and so  that's really what led me to the idea of  writing a book about his about his life  and works okay in your books title used  a term adverse genres what do you mean  by that this is going to sound like the  title of a Yale professor I invented the  term but the term came out of my reading  of his works and the idea of adverse  Shinra is that of a writer who uses a  particular genre like an ode or a sonnet  or a lyrical poem yet who fills it with  content that doesn't belong to the genre  that's dramatically imported as it were  from another kind of genre so we might  have a lyric poet poem written  dramatically for example and pessoas  work is full of these examples his  various hetero atoms or personalities  that he invented to write each select  different genres from the tradition of  Western literature but the expression is  not one that belongs really to the genre  that's selected I found it very  interesting thesis done at Yale on  Mozart which makes the point that Mozart  changed the classical genre  by injecting motifs from German opera  from other sources that didn't really  belong to the classical genre yet  because of that he was so original and  ended up influencing the very course of  classicism itself something parallel to  that and  terms of pistolas importing many  different kinds of genres from different  moments in Western literature and  changing their fundamental nature that's  what I mean by the adverse genres and  can you give an explanation as to why he  would do that  yes he's his devotion to his literary  project was total and he had the idea  also not unusual in literary modernism  that he wanted to be a universal writer  and fact he didn't want to be that just  by writing many different kinds of works  he wanted to write whole literature's or  an entire literature as he put it  so his dispersion dispersing of his work  and too many different hetero names and  the very diverse nature of it I think is  aimed at producing an entire literature  okay and let's talk a little bit about  your research how did you go about  gathering the data for your book since  this book is mostly an interpretation  the answers are very simple at Sterling  Memorial Library the the burro direct  interlibrary loan and the by Nicki  of course I had to try to get acquainted  with a very very large bibliography on  the works of pessoa  particularly works that might bear on my  own and the number of publications the  do biography is really immense on pencil  plus then I needed to find several books  that that we might not have had in the  library and so forth but it was all  mostly printed resources this book  doesn't rely on any kind of archival  work it's a pretty straight  interpretation in your book you states  that pessoa is Portugal's greatest poet  and one of the most prominent figures in  European modernism how so  certainly in terms of Portuguese  literature the Suez importance is  undeniable because of the great interest  in this works I often tell my students  said if Portugal could change the  country's name to something else they've  changed it to pessoa in a way because  pessoa wrote in and represented because  of the division of his work all of the  major directions of literary  tourism that we find and many of the  other literature's the ratio of the  author the multiplication of personality  the difference between biography and  works all of those features and many  others we find in Pasillas works such  that it's a never-ending source of  interest for for his readers the other  interesting thing about pessoa is that  he published relatively little during  his lifetime and he left 25,000 sheets  of manuscript paper in a wooden trunk  that was found after his death and which  is now in the National Library of  Portugal and their teams of researchers  going through these 25,000 pages to try  to decide in what order and where all of  this belongs in terms of Pasillas work  so he was able to project his work as it  were beyond the grave so that people are  still trying to put together and  discover exactly what he wrote and what  it meant do you have any plans yourself  to read through those papers if they  become available I think it would be  very interesting so far I've just read  what other people have to say about the  papers his handwriting was enormous ly  difficult to decipher and so the experts  have devoted many years to it I think  I'm going to leave most of it to them  but it would be really fascinating to  see some of his manuscript sheets in the  future I bet it would okay final  question what what was one of the most  surprising things you found in doing the  research for the book I think I gained  an appreciation that I had never seen  before in spite of teaching pessoa of  the depth of his imagination and of the  deep significance of the many different  ways in which he wrote and in fact found  that he had written in my opinion at  least three or four have some of the  greatest works of 20th century modernism  there were other very interesting  details I found out that his quatrains  of popular Virts he wrote hundreds of  them in a typical Portuguese style had  been translated to English by a  professor named Philip become rich of  moorland state university so I was able  to get in touch with professor come rich  and get his permission to use some of  his very clever translations in  my book and I also was interested to  find out the depth of pessoas influence  from other lesser-known works his play  the Mariner which is a play in which  there's no action whatsoever is based on  theories of static theatre that come  from moeder link Morris Maurice  Maeterlinck was a Belgian playwright and  author and in the book of disquietude of  pessoa he mentions a Swiss memorialist  named Emil Omri Frederick Emil and I was  able to find his intimate journal his  journaling team and dozens of volumes  and trace the origins of a lot of  Portuguese a lot of pessoas lines and  his book of disquiet to to Emile's  journal so this kind of literary  correspondence was revealing and I think  was one of the surprising features of  the research I did for the book it's  wonderful thank you so much for being  with us thank you for your invitation a  pleasure to talk with you for more  information about professor Jackson and  his work please visit our website at  yale.edu backslash macmillan report be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you
 

